kangaroo-at-2018-kadett-25.body:
Take one grid square as the unit. All four arcs are quarter circles of radius 2. The two wing arcs bulge **outwards** and the two tail arcs bulge **inwards**, and each piece between an arc and its chord has the same area. So we may replace every arc by its chord without changing the area.

The dove becomes a hexagon with corners (counted in squares from the top-left corner of the flag) $(0,0), (2,2), (4,0), (6,2), (4,4), (1,3)$. The white part of the $6 \times 4$ flag is then five triangles:
- between the wings: base 4, height 2, area 4;
- top right corner: legs 2 and 2, area 2;
- bottom right corner: legs 2 and 2, area 2;
- below the dove: base 4 (bottom edge), height 1, area 2;
- left of the dove: base 4 (left edge), height 1, area 2.

White area $= 12$, so the dove covers $24 - 12 = 12$ squares. Each square has area $192 : 12 = 16$ cm², i.e. side 4 cm, and the flag measures $6 \cdot 4 \times 4 \cdot 4 = 24 \text{ cm} \times 16 \text{ cm}$.

**Answer: (D) 24 cm × 16 cm**
